@page "/learn/quickgrid-onrowclick"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Clickable grid rows · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Clickable grid rows</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">People click a row to open it. How do you know which row they clicked?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Grids &amp; lists · Interactive Server · about 4 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> QuickGrid has <code>OnRowClick</code>, which runs your code when someone
        clicks a row. In .NET 10 rows had no click event. Only a button or element you added inside a cell could
        be clicked.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below are the eight most recently active support tickets. In .NET 10 a row can't be clicked, so each row
        gets an <b>Open</b> button:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The .NET 10 grid</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;QuickGrid Items="tickets" RowClass="Highlight"&gt;
    &lt;PropertyColumn Title="Ticket" Property="t =&gt; t.Id" Format="'#'0" /&gt;
    &lt;PropertyColumn Title="Subject" Property="t =&gt; t.Subject" /&gt;
    &lt;PropertyColumn Title="Customer" Property="t =&gt; t.Company" /&gt;
    &lt;TemplateColumn&gt;
        <span class="err">&lt;button type="button" class="open" @@onclick="() =&gt; OpenAsync(context)"&gt;Open&lt;/button&gt;</span>
    &lt;/TemplateColumn&gt;
&lt;/QuickGrid&gt;</pre>
    </div>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="A button in every row" Url="demo/row-click/tickets/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: a button in every row" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Click a subject or a customer. Nothing opens. Only the <b>Open</b> button opens a ticket.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>Rows can't be clicked</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 10, QuickGrid gives you no way to add a click to a row. Inside the grid, the only place
        for a click handler is a cell, using a <code>TemplateColumn</code>:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Where a click can land, and what it does</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;tr&gt;                       <span class="dim">no handler</span>
├─ &lt;td&gt; Ticket              <span class="err">nothing happens</span>
├─ &lt;td&gt; Subject             <span class="err">nothing happens</span>
├─ &lt;td&gt; Customer            <span class="err">nothing happens</span>
└─ &lt;td&gt; &lt;button @@onclick&gt;  <span class="ok">✓ opens the ticket</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        So most of the row does nothing. The button works, but it's a small target and an extra column on every
        list.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Add OnRowClick</h2>
    <p>Set <code>OnRowClick</code> to a method that takes the row's item.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;QuickGrid <span class="hl">TGridItem="TicketRow"</span> Items="tickets"
           <span class="hl">OnRowClick="OpenAsync"</span>&gt;
    ...
&lt;/QuickGrid&gt;

<span class="hl">async Task OpenAsync(TicketRow row)</span>
{
    open = await Desk.GetAsync(row.Id);
}</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        QuickGrid listens for clicks on every row and passes your method the row's item. <code>OpenAsync</code> is
        the same method the button called. Only the wiring changes.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Don't leave out TGridItem</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;QuickGrid Items="tickets" OnRowClick="OpenAsync"&gt;</pre>
        <div class="verdict bad">✗ CS1503: the grid can't work out the row type from the method alone. Name it with <code>TGridItem="TicketRow"</code>.</div>
    </div>
    <p>
        One catch: a row can't be reached with the keyboard, so <code>OnRowClick</code> only helps mouse users.
        Fig. 2 drops the button to keep things simple. In a real app, keep a link or button in the row as well.
        The Support screen makes each subject a link.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same list, with OnRowClick" Url="demo/row-click/tickets"
                Label=".NET 11: OnRowClick">
        <Caption>
            Click anywhere on a row. The ticket opens below the grid, and its row stays highlighted.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here's the code for both lists:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/RowClick/TicketListNet10.razor" Feature="quickgrid-onrowclick">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1's list. The button in the <code>TemplateColumn</code> is the only way to call <code>OpenAsync</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/RowClick/TicketList.razor" Feature="quickgrid-onrowclick">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's list. Lines 7 and 8 add <code>TGridItem</code> and <code>OnRowClick</code>, and the button
            column is gone. <code>OpenAsync</code> is unchanged from Fig. 3.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Clicks inside the cells</h2>
    <p>With no row click, the handler had to go inside a cell. Here's what we tried on .NET 10:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three ways to try it in .NET 10, and what clicking the row did</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;TemplateColumn&gt;
  &lt;button @@onclick="..."&gt;Open&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/TemplateColumn&gt;</pre>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ clicking the subject or customer does nothing; only the button opens it</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;TemplateColumn Title="Subject"&gt;
  &lt;div <span class="err">@@onclick="..."</span>&gt;...&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/TemplateColumn&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">every column becomes a <code>TemplateColumn</code>, each with its own handler</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ the cell's padding is dead: only the text area opens it</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;QuickGrid TGridItem="TicketRow"
  <span class="err">OnRowClick="OpenAsync"</span>&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">the .NET 11 markup on .NET 10. QuickGrid there doesn't know <code>OnRowClick</code>, so it copies it onto the <code>&lt;table&gt;</code></span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ builds with no warning, renders a dead <code>onrowclick</code> attribute, and clicks do nothing</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        There were two other options outside the grid. One was JavaScript: tag rows with
        <code>RowClass</code>, catch the clicks in JS and call back into .NET, plus cleanup code you must not forget. The other was CSS: stretch one link in each
        row to cover the whole row with <code>::after</code>. That works with the keyboard, but you add the CSS to
        every grid, and any other button in the row has to sit above the link to stay clickable.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>Now it's a real parameter</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11, <code>OnRowClick</code> is a real QuickGrid parameter, so the grid calls your method. If you
        see an <code>onrowclick</code> attribute in the page's HTML, you're still on .NET 10.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Links or buttons inside a row?</b> Add <code>@@onclick:stopPropagation</code> to them. Otherwise a click on one opens the row too.</li>
        <li><b>It needs an interactive render mode.</b> On a normal static page the rows still render, but clicking them does nothing.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
